  "title": "CHAPTER 5: BANKING IN INDIA",
  "order": 225,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "gbog7yt",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. INTRODUCTION TO BANKING"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Commercial banks operate by mobilizing public savings through deposits and granting loans to generate credit in the economy.",
        "Bank nationalisation was executed in two major phases: 14 major banks in 1969 and 6 additional banks in 1980.",
        "An asset becomes a Non-Performing Asset (NPA) when interest or principal payments remain overdue for more than 90 days.",
        "Priority Sector Lending (PSL) ensures adequate credit flows to key vulnerable sectors including agriculture, MSMEs, and education.",
        "Capital Adequacy Ratio (CAR) and Basel Norms serve as financial safety buffers to absorb potential bank losses."
      ],
      "id": "4v6avvb"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Nationalisation Phases",
          "value": "1969 & 1980",
